WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.205
Six steps, in a line: banana,
strawberries, milk, lid, blend, pour.

2
00:00:10.425 --> 00:00:15.858
Follow it. Understand it. Edit it.
Correct it. Four things, one algorithm.

3
00:00:16.358 --> 00:00:21.838
A linear algorithm is a line of steps:
a first, a next, a next, and an end.

4
00:00:22.058 --> 00:00:27.910
Making a smoothie is linear. Banana
first, then strawberries, then milk.

5
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:34.074
Then the lid on, then blend, then
pour. Six steps, one after another.

6
00:00:34.294 --> 00:00:38.056
You go top to bottom. You
never jump back up the list.

7
00:00:38.276 --> 00:00:43.477
Linear does not mean short. It means in
order, one after another, and it can be long.

8
00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:47.739
Following an algorithm means
doing the step it names next.

9
00:00:47.959 --> 00:00:52.928
And only that step. The algorithm
says lid, so you put the lid on.

10
00:00:53.148 --> 00:00:57.281
Not blend, even if you would like
to. Blend comes after the lid.

11
00:00:57.501 --> 00:01:03.027
A computer does exactly this. A wrong
step makes a wrong thing happen.

12
00:01:03.527 --> 00:01:07.753
Understanding is not the same as
following. It means knowing why.

13
00:01:07.973 --> 00:01:10.620
Why does the lid go on
before you blend it?

14
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.973
Take the lid step out and blend
anyway. Smoothie round the kitchen!

15
00:01:15.193 --> 00:01:18.909
Pour last, because you pour what
the blender has made smooth.

16
00:01:19.129 --> 00:01:24.562
Every step is where it is for a reason.
That reasoning is logical thinking.

17
00:01:25.062 --> 00:01:30.496
Sometimes a step is wrong. Correcting
means putting the right step in its place.

18
00:01:30.716 --> 00:01:36.985
This kite algorithm says: cross the sticks,
tie them, cut the paper into tiny pieces.

19
00:01:37.205 --> 00:01:42.778
Tiny pieces? The paper has to cover
the sticks. That step is the bug.

20
00:01:42.998 --> 00:01:46.899
Take it out and put the right step
in. Glue paper over the sticks.

21
00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:50.741
Now run the algorithm again
and check. The kite flies!

22
00:01:51.241 --> 00:01:56.210
Editing is not correcting. Nothing is
wrong: you want something different.

23
00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:00.935
This tower algorithm is big brick,
middle brick, small brick, flag.

24
00:02:01.155 --> 00:02:05.288
You want a red top. Change the
small brick step to a red brick.

25
00:02:05.508 --> 00:02:10.245
One step changed, and the tower
is different. A red brick on top!

26
00:02:10.465 --> 00:02:14.087
Edit is a change you want.
Correct is a fix you need.

27
00:02:14.587 --> 00:02:17.838
There are four things you
can do with one algorithm.

28
00:02:18.058 --> 00:02:24.560
Follow: do the steps. Understand:
know why each step is there.

29
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:31.235
Edit: change it because you want to.
Correct: fix a step that is wrong.

30
00:02:31.455 --> 00:02:36.424
Swapping jam for honey because you
like honey is editing, not correcting.

31
00:02:36.924 --> 00:02:41.103
A linear algorithm is a line
of steps, from top to bottom.

32
00:02:41.323 --> 00:02:45.642
You follow it exactly, and you
understand why each step is there.

33
00:02:45.862 --> 00:02:50.460
You edit it when you want a change,
and correct it when a step is wrong.
